---
import FaqBlock from '../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Romance Guide - 12 Options, Killer Risk & Route Tracker';
const description =
  'Pre-launch Grave Seasons romance guide built from official Steam and Blumhouse facts: 12 romance slots, changing killer context, and how to assess relationship advice.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Romance Guide', url: '/romance-guide/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steam, SOURCES.blumhouse, SOURCES.blumhouseMedia];
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How many romance options are confirmed for Grave Seasons?',
    answer:
      'Steam states that Grave Seasons lets players romance one of 12 potential folks that capture their heart.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are gift preferences and heart events officially published?',
    answer:
      'No. Gift preferences, event triggers, and exact dialogue routes have not been published by Steam or Blumhouse yet, so this page does not invent them.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a romance option be the killer?',
    answer:
      'Steam says a few romanceable folks have the potential to be the killer. It also says every playthrough selects a supernatural killer from a pool of candidates.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When will this become a full romance route guide?',
    answer:
      'After release, this page should be updated with verified in-game routes, gifts, event triggers, and spoiler-labeled outcomes.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.yuto.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Romance guide</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      Grave Seasons Romance Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      Grave Seasons has 12 potential romance options, and Steam warns that a few of them may be the killer. The game is not out until March 9, 2027, so this page sticks to what is official now and explains how to track relationships safely once you can play.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-[1.2fr_0.8fr] md:items-start">
      <div class="prose-page">
        <h2>Confirmed romance facts</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Steam says Grave Seasons includes 12 potential romanceable people.</li>
          <li>Steam says a few romanceable people may have the potential to be the killer.</li>
          <li>Steam says every playthrough selects a supernatural killer from a pool of candidates.</li>
          <li>Steam frames the town as Ashenridge, with 30+ residents and 70+ interiors.</li>
          <li>Blumhouse describes the game as relationship building and crop harvesting mixed with finding the supernatural serial killer.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What is not confirmed yet</h2>
        <p>
          There is no official gift chart, heart-event schedule, dialogue route, marriage route, breakup system, birthday list, or complete resident roster at this time. Any page claiming full routes before launch should be treated as speculative unless it shows direct in-game evidence.
        </p>

        <h2>Romance system baseline</h2>
        <p>
          The official pages frame romance as part of a wider relationship and murder mystery system. Steam confirms 12 potential romanceable people and warns that a few may have the potential to be the killer, so this guide treats romance routing and killer identification as connected systems rather than separate checklists.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Relationship building is part of the central game pitch.</li>
          <li>Romance exists alongside farming, foraging, crafting, and investigation.</li>
          <li>Deaths and survival can unlock different storylines.</li>
          <li>The selected killer changes the campaign, targets, and murder style.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <img
          src={ASSETS.yuto.src}
          alt={ASSETS.yuto.alt}
          width={ASSETS.yuto.width}
          height={ASSETS.yuto.height}
          class="rounded-md border border-white/10"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">
          Official Blumhouse gallery caption references Yuto, but does not publish romance route details.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <section class="prose-page mt-10">
      <h2>Why the changing killer matters for romance</h2>
      <p>Steam describes a candidate pool for the killer and explicitly allows romance with someone while they are a killer or a victim. That makes a relationship guide dependent on the current story context: advice that describes one run cannot automatically describe the same person's role in another. This is a consequence of the announced design, not a tested route rule.</p>
      <p>The twelve romance slots also differ from the town's 30+ residents. Being named in a screenshot does not establish that a character fills one of those slots. Yuto and Hari are identifiable in official media, but that alone cannot confirm either a romance route or membership in the killer pool.</p>
      <h2>How to judge a romance guide</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A gift recommendation needs an observed response. No preferred-gift list is established by the store description.</li>
        <li>A route walkthrough needs story context and spoiler labels. A character being alive, a victim, or the killer can change the context the store describes.</li>
        <li>Heart thresholds, marriage, exclusivity, and recoverable dialogue mistakes need playable evidence; none follows from the count of twelve.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Use the <a href="/characters/">character overview</a> for identified names and the <a href="/killer-decisions-and-replay/">investigation guide</a> for the difference between an observation and a suspect theory. These are useful boundaries for reading previews today, without treating an untested route as established advice.</p>
    </section>

    <FaqBlock items={faqs} />
    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
